# Hook

<So I decided I was going to cook hibachi.
I’ve got the chicken out. The vegetables. The rice.
I roll an egg across the griddle and say, to absolutely nobody,
“There’s my eggroll.”
I stack up some onions. Make my little volcano.
Pour some oil in it. Try to light it.
Nothing.
Draw a little smiley face with the oil. Try that.
Nothing.
But the griddle’s hot. I put the chicken down.
It sizzles. I squirt some teriyaki on it.
Now we’re getting somewhere.
I let it cook, get my spatula underneath it.
I’m about to make this flip look good.
It doesn’t move.
I push a little harder.
The top half moves.
The bottom half has become one with the Blackstone.
“Hey, babe! We’re having shredded chicken tonight.”
Then come the vegetables.
Some of them fry. Some of them attach themselves permanently.
So now, instead of flipping, I’m scraping.
Tessa opens the door.
“What’s that sound?”
“That’s just Hibachi Kyle at work.”
The rice wasn’t any better.
It just accepted that it lived there now.
My spatula’s going across the top.
There’s a whole layer underneath that’s not coming with us.
I don’t even know why I tried the eggs.
By the time I finished, they were a breakfast memorial.
Nothing flipped through the air.
Nothing danced across the griddle.
There wasn’t any choo-choo train.
Just me. Scraping dinner.
But I told myself, “It’s probably not that bad.”
Boy, was I wrong.
I bring it inside.
Tessa, bless her heart, says it’s good.
She loves me.
Then she asks, “Did you season it?”
“I seasoned the rice.”
That was not the seasoning she meant.

But here’s the part I keep coming back to.
The dinner was a wreck.
And Tessa still told me she loved me.
She said it with a mouth full of shredded chicken.
Her word about me was not up for a vote.
It just didn’t cool that griddle down one degree.

I keep expecting those two things to travel together.
If I’m loved, the flip ought to look good.
If somebody is pleased with me, dinner ought to come off clean.

That’s not how it went in my kitchen.
And that is not how it goes in Mark 1.

Turn there with me.
Jesus comes up out of the water.
Heaven is torn open. The Spirit comes down on Him like a dove.
And the Father says it out loud:
“You are my Son, whom I love; with you I am well pleased.”

That is the best word anybody has ever heard.
Now look at what Mark sets right beside it.
“At once the Spirit sent him out into the wilderness.”
Not next year. At once.
Forty days. Tempted by Satan. Wild animals.
And angels attended Him.

Now hear me carefully, because this is where we usually get it backwards.
Mark does not say Jesus went out there because He wasn’t ready.
Mark says the opposite.
Before one day in that desert,
before Satan said one word to Him,
heaven had already called Him beloved.
The wilderness is not where He earned that.
It is where He carried it.

So the testing was not heaven’s verdict on Him.
The Father spoke first.
Then the hard place came.
And what the Father said was still true when it did.

[Revision note: this hook previously landed on skipping preparation, which would have implied Jesus
entered the wilderness unready. Mark 1:9-13 gives the reverse order. The Father's affirmation comes
in v.11, before the wilderness, and Mark reports no failure between the two. The turn is now
unexpected testing after affirmation.

Quotations are NIV, checked against the chapter text in `Mark 1.md`: v.11 "You are my Son, whom I
love; with you I am well pleased"; v.12 "At once the Spirit sent him out into the wilderness"; v.13
forty days, tempted by Satan, with the wild animals, and angels attended him. Tessa's word standing
while the griddle stayed hot is the illustration's own logic, not a claim that the Father's word is
comparable to a kindness at the table.]
